Simple hematoma from collision with fixed post to forehead
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Localized dome-shaped frontal swelling, Intact overlying skin, Blunt force collision with preserved neurological baseline

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-017
Reason for Visit: Rapidly expanding mass on the forehead following a collision, requiring urgent clearance of skull fracture or intracranial injury.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a direct frontal head strike resulting in immediate, significant localized swelling. Urgent evaluation was necessary to assess for frontal bone fracture, intracranial pathology, and ocular involvement before morning clinical services were available.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I walked directly into a wooden post in the dark, and my forehead immediately blew up like a balloon.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a clear history of a direct, blunt, low-velocity collision with a stationary vertical object. This resulted in a rapidly expanding, localized dome-shaped subcutaneous swelling strictly confined to the point of impact on the forehead. The patient notes discoloration ranging from red to purplish-blue under the intact skin. Focal tenderness is strictly isolated to the swollen area without widespread facial pain. The patient forcefully denies any loss of consciousness, amnesia, visual changes, or neck pain.
Functional Status: Fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Poor lighting in the hallway contributed to the collision.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/82 | HR: 80 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, with a prominent but localized forehead swelling.
Physical Exam: Facial examination reveals a localized dome-shaped frontal swelling with intact overlying skin. The swelling has a firm, tense consistency with mild central fluctuance, consistent with an organizing hematoma. Palpation of the perimeter confirms intact underlying bone. There is a complete absence of periorbital ecchymosis (raccoon eyes) extending below the brow line. Visual acuity and extraocular movements are normal with an absence of diplopia. Gross neurological examination is entirely normal (GCS 15).
Environmental Observations: Hallway light bulb is burnt out; advised replacement.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications. No anticoagulants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Simple hematoma to forehead - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Frontal bone fracture - Ruled out due to: Absence of palpable bony step-off beneath the hematoma and intact structural contour of the superior orbital rim.

Intracranial hemorrhage - Ruled out due to: Preserved neurological baseline, GCS 15, normal pupillary response, and lack of systemic symptoms (vomiting/severe headache).

Orbital blowout fracture - Ruled out due to: Normal extraocular muscle movements, absence of diplopia, and intact sensation over the cheek (infraorbital nerve).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The presentation of a rapidly forming "goosebump" on the forehead following blunt trauma inherently requires differentiation from a depressed skull fracture or an epidural hematoma. Thorough bedside neurological and physical assessment confidently isolated the pathology to a superficial subcutaneous hematoma with an intact cranium. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically required to clear the patient of severe intracranial injury, provide necessary reassurance, and implement acute localized compression therapy, safely avoiding unnecessary radiation exposure from a CT scan at the emergency department.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Recommended conservative management with cold compresses. Advised against needle aspiration due to the high risk of introducing bacterial infection into a sterile hematoma.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a cold pack wrapped in a towel for 15 minutes every 1-2 hours. The cold induces local vasoconstriction, which directly stops further bleeding into the tissue space and rapidly decreases the tense, throbbing pain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Reusable cold pack.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks only if the lump fails to begin shrinking or becomes painful and hot.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department if the swelling suddenly increases in size, if you develop a severe escalating headache, or if you begin vomiting repetitively.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Subcutaneous hematomas on the forehead often track downward due to gravity, causing bruising around the eyes in the coming days.
Clinical Course: The lump will become firmer over the next week before slowly reabsorbing over 2 to 4 weeks. The bruising will change color from purple to green to yellow.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if your vision changes, if the lump becomes hot and exquisitely painful, or if you feel increasingly confused or drowsy.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The lump is a localized collection of blood between the skin and the skull. The skull is perfectly intact underneath it.
Actionable Steps: Use ice packs frequently today. Sleep with an extra pillow tonight. Do not push forcefully on the lump to try and flatten it.

